
---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Permissions"
  description="Learn how Loot Containers configuration permissions work, including operator access, allowlists, and authorization."
>
  <div class="doc-section">
    <span class="eyebrow">CONFIGURATION</span>
    <h1>Permissions</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Control who can access and manage Loot Containers configuration.
      Permission settings are available through the in-game configuration screen.
    </p>

    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Loot Containers includes several permission settings designed to control
      access to configuration management. These include operator requirements,
      an optional player allowlist, and an operator bypass option.
    </p>

    <p>
      The configuration screen currently provides controls for all these
      settings. However, only the server-side operator permission check is
      currently enforced. The configurable allowlist and permission toggles
      have not yet been connected to the server's authorization system.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Current authorization behavior</strong>
      <p>
        Players must have Minecraft operator permission level 2 or higher
        to receive configuration data and save configuration changes.
        This requirement is currently enforced regardless of the permission
        toggle settings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Permission Settings</h2>
    <p>
      The Permissions section of the in-game configuration screen contains
      three toggle options and an allowlist management section.
    </p>

    <h3>Require OP</h3>
    <p>
      Determines whether operator status should be required for configuration
      access when the allowlist is disabled.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>ON:</strong> Intended to require operator permissions.</li>
      <li><strong>OFF:</strong> Intended to remove that operator requirement.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Current implementation:</strong> The server always checks
      for operator permission level 2 or higher. Changing this setting
      does not currently change that requirement.
    </p>

    <h3>Allowlist</h3>
    <p>
      Enables the allowlist option, which is designed to restrict configuration
      access to specific players.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>ON:</strong> Intended to enable allowlist-based access.</li>
      <li><strong>OFF:</strong> Disables the allowlist option.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Current implementation:</strong> The configuration screen
      supports adding and removing player UUIDs, but the server does not
      currently check the allowlist when authorizing configuration requests.
    </p>

    <h3>OP Bypass</h3>
    <p>
      Configures whether operators should be able to bypass allowlist
      restrictions.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>ON:</strong> Intended to allow operators to bypass the allowlist.</li>
      <li><strong>OFF:</strong> Intended to prevent that bypass.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Current implementation:</strong> This setting is not currently
      connected to the server's authorization checks.
    </p>

    <h2>Allowlist Management</h2>
    <p>
      The configuration screen provides tools for managing the list of
      permitted player UUIDs.
    </p>

    <h3>Adding a Player</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the Permissions section.</li>
      <li>Enter the player's Minecraft UUID in the Player UUID field.</li>
      <li>Select <strong>Add UUID</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      UUID entries must use a valid UUID format. The input field accepts
      up to 36 characters, and invalid UUIDs are rejected. Duplicate
      entries are also prevented.
    </p>

    <h3>Removing a Player</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the UUID of the player you want to remove.</li>
      <li>Select <strong>Remove UUID</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      UUID removal uses case-insensitive matching. The screen displays
      shortened UUID entries, showing up to five players at a time.
      If additional players exist, the interface displays how many
      more entries are in the list.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Important: Allowlist enforcement is not implemented yet</strong>
      <p>
        Adding a UUID stores it in the configuration, but it does not
        currently grant or deny access to the configuration editor.
        The server still uses its operator permission level 2 check.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Default Values</h2>
    <p>
      The following values are initialized when LootSettings is created.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Setting</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Require OP</td>
            <td><code>true</code></td>
            <td>Operator requirement toggle.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Allowlist Enabled</td>
            <td><code>false</code></td>
            <td>Allowlist toggle.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>OP Bypass</td>
            <td><code>true</code></td>
            <td>Operator bypass toggle.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Allowed Players</td>
            <td>Empty</td>
            <td>List of stored player UUIDs.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Current Server Authorization</h2>
    <p>
      Configuration access is checked by the server when a player requests
      configuration synchronization or submits configuration changes.
    </p>

    <h3>Opening the Configuration Editor</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>
        The client requests configuration synchronization from the server.
      </li>
      <li>
        The server checks whether the player has operator permission
        level 2 or higher.
      </li>
      <li>
        If authorized, the server sends permission approval and configuration
        data to that player.
      </li>
      <li>
        The client opens the editor after receiving the configuration data
        and confirming its local permission state.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Saving Configuration</h3>
    <p>
      When a player submits configuration changes, the server performs
      another operator permission check. Players who do not meet the
      requirement have their save requests rejected.
    </p>

    <p>
      Authorized configuration changes are validated before being saved.
      If a save fails, the server attempts to restore the previous
      configuration and reports the result to the player.
    </p>

    <div class="code-card">
      <strong>Current authorization check</strong>
      <pre><code>{`if (player == null || !player.hasPermissions(2)) {
    return;
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        This check is used for both configuration synchronization
        and configuration saving.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Resetting Permissions</h2>
    <p>
      The Permissions section includes reset behavior that restores
      the following values:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Require OP: ON</li>
      <li>Allowlist: OFF</li>
      <li>OP Bypass: ON</li>
      <li>Allowed Players: Empty</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Resetting these values restores the configuration settings,
      but does not change the current server-side authorization behavior.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>Can non-operators currently open the configuration editor?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The server requires operator permission level 2 or higher
      before sending configuration data.
    </p>

    <h3>Does enabling the allowlist restrict access?</h3>
    <p>
      Not currently. The interface supports managing UUIDs, but the
      server does not yet use the allowlist to authorize players.
    </p>

    <h3>Does disabling Require OP remove the operator requirement?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The server currently enforces the operator permission level
      2 check independently of this setting.
    </p>

    <h3>Can operators bypass the allowlist?</h3>
    <p>
      The OP Bypass setting is available in the configuration screen,
      but its behavior has not yet been implemented in server-side
      authorization.
    </p>

    <h3>Where are permission settings saved?</h3>
    <p>
      Permission settings are part of LootSettings and are saved through
      the mod's general configuration manager.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
